          %p
            Important examples of monads come from “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+object"} free
            structures”. For instance, there is the “free
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo :
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Set
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T : \mathbf{Set} \to \mathbf{Set}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TX
            is (the underlying set of) the real vector space generated by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
            of sets,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tf
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tf
            is the unique linear function from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TX
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TY
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TY
            that coincides with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            when restricted to the basis
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi TX
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TX
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            occurs in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TX
            as a generating set (hence a function
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi TX
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_X : X \to TX
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo +
                  %mi tz
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi rx
                  %mo +
                  %mi rsy
                  %mo +
                  %mi rst
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r(x + s(y+tz)) = rx + rsy + rst z
            (hence a function
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi TTX
                    %mo →
                    %mi TX
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_X : TTX \to TX
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TX
            is just a set—its algebraic structure is encoded by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_X
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_X
          %p
            Together with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"}> adjoint functors
            ) that they correspond to (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#RelationBetweenAdjunctionsAndMonads"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            (where they form the basis of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+algebra"} categorical
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+algebra"} universal algebra
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+over+a+monad"} algebras over a monad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            more generally (certainly in fields like
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+and+topos+theory"} sheaf and topos theory
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            = succeed "\u201D)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+resolutions"} canonical resolutions
          %p
            Last but not least, monads play a central role in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            (cf.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computer+science"} computer science
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+trilogy"} computational trilogy
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+programming"} functional programming
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+%28computer+science%29"} monads in computer science
          %h2#Etymology Etymology
          %p
            The term “monad” was introduced in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Bénabou67"} Bénabou 1967, Def. 5.4.1
            %a{:href => "delooping#DeloopingOfHigherCategoricalStructures"} deloopings of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Benabou-MonoidsAsMonads.jpg", :width => "700px"}/
          %p#BenabouFootnote it says in a footnote:
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Benabou-FootnoteOnMonadTerminology.jpg", :width => "700px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Bénabou67"} Bénabou 1967, p. 40
          %p
            Beyond this footnote, the only contemporary account that seems to exist of the terminological genesis,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Barr09"} Barr 2009
          %blockquote
            %p In the summer (or maybe late spring, the Oberwohlfach records will show this) of 1966, there was a category meeting there. &amp;lbrack;…&amp;rbrack; One day at lunch or dinner I happened to be sitting next to Jean Bénabou and he turned to me and said something like “How about `monad'?" I thought about and said it sounded pretty good to me. So Jean proposed it to the general audience and there was general agreement. It suggested "monoid" of course and it is a monoid in a functor category.
          %p#MonadTerminologyFurtherDiscussion
            Further discussion on the issue in 2023 has recollections by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jir%C3%AD+Ad%C3%A1mek"} Jirí Adámek
            of recollections by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"} Bill Lawvere
            to the extent that:
          %blockquote
            %p
              it was in the common room of the old castle at Oberwolfach when Sammy &amp;lbrack;
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"}> Samuel Eilenberg
              \&amp;rbrack; came out from behind the piano and announced the change.
          %p
            But
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Barr"} Michael Barr
            clarifies that:
          %blockquote
            %p I am more than willing to believe that it was Bénabou sitting next to me who proposed monad. It is entirely possible that Sammy came down and pronounced it “official”. And it was certainly in the old castle.
          %p
            Interestingly Bénabou’s footnote
            %a{:href => "#BenabouFootnote"} above
            gives a second motivation for “monad”:
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Benabou-MonadDefinition.jpg", :width => "650px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Bénabou67"} Bénabou 1967, p. 39
          %p#ItIsStriking
            It is striking that
            %a{:href => "#Bénabou67"} Bénabou 1967, Def. 5.4.1
            %em defines
            a monad to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+2-functor"} lax 2-functor
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+category"} terminal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} 2-category of categories
            (and more generally to whatever given ambient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi S
                    %mo ̲
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{S}
            ”) and then proceeds to unwind the equivalence of this definition to the traditional one:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Monads
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %munder
                    %mi S
                    %mo ̲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} {
                  %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi lax
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mi S
                    %mo ̲
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Monads(\underline{S})
                  \\;\;\;
                  \\simeq
                  \\;\;\;
                  \\Big\{
                  1 \xrightarrow{\; lax \;} \underline{S}
                  \\Big\}
                  \\,.
          %p
            In this sense, monads are
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+element"} point-like elements
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+theory"} 2-category theoretic
            sense (say in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-topos"} 2-topos
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %a{:href => "monad+terminology#HistoricalOrigins"} Euclid’s ancient notion of monads
            as indivisible building blocks. In fact, as discussed there, “monad” (both in ancient and still in modern Greek) just means “unit” in the sense of the unit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %a{:href => "#Bénabou67"} Bénabou 1967, Def. 5.4.1
            literally identifies monads with the (lax) units
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %munder
                    %mi S
                    %mo ̲
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to \underline{S}
            in the ambient 2-category.
          %p
            In generalization of this situation, one may consider lax functors out of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codiscrete+groupoids"} codiscrete groupoids
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CoDisc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊔
                        %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CoDisc(1^{\sqcup_n})
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            objects, which
            %a{:href => "#Bénabou67"} Bénabou 1967, Def. 5.5
            calls
            = succeed ":" do
              %em polyads
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Polyads
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %munder
                    %mi S
                    %mo ̲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} {
                  %mi CoDisc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊔
                        %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi lax
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mi S
                    %mo ̲
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Polyads(\underline{S})
                  \\;\;\;
                  \\simeq
                  \\;\;\;
                  \\Big\{
                  CoDisc(1^{\sqcup_n})
                  \\xrightarrow{\; lax \;}
                  \\underline{S}
                  \\Big\}
                  \\,.
          %p
            On the other hand (as maybe alluded to in the first line of
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Barr09"} Barr 2009
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+terminology"} monad terminology
            had already been adopted in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonstandard+analysis"} nonstandard analysis
            as the term for
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhoods"} infinitesimal neighbourhoods
            (
            %a{:href => "infinitesimal+neighborhood#Robinson66"} Robinson 1966, p. 57
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#infinitesimal+neighborhood#Luxemburg66"} Luxembourg 1966
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "infinitesimal+neighborhood#Keisler76"} Keisler 1976, Def. 1.2
            %a{:href => "infinitesimal+neighborhood#Kutateladze11"} Kutateladze 2011
            and, speaking
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetically
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "infinitesimal+neighborhood#Kock80"} Kock 1980
          %p
            It so happens that — in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theoretic
            formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimals"} infinitesimals
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential cohesion
            — the construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhoods"} infinitesimal neighbourhoods
            %em is
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "infinitesimal+disk+bundle#MonadicityAdjointToJetBundles"} here
            = succeed "!" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+monad"} adjoint monad
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+comonad"} jet comonad
            (
            %a{:href => "infinitesimal+disk+bundle#KhavkineSchreiber17"}> Khavkine &amp; Schreiber 2017, p. 23
            ).
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#monads Monads
          %p
            A
            %strong monad
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is given by
          %ol
