            commute (where certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence"} coherence
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
          %p
            The name “monad” and the terms “unit”, “multiplication” and “associativity” bear a clear analogy with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            (but see also at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+%28disambiguation%29"} monad (disambiguation)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            as just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
            in the endomorphism category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(a,a)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+in+a+monoidal+category"} monoid in a monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            can be defined as a monad in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B} C
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            A third and somewhat less obvious definition says that a monad in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+2-functor"} lax 2-functor
            from the terminal bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            to
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is sent to the object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_a
            becomes
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            arise from the coherent 2-cells expressing lax functoriality. This, in turn, is equivalent to saying that a monad is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+enriched+in+a+bicategory"} category enriched in a bicategory
            with a single object and single morphism. Among higher-category theorists, it is tempting to suggest that this is the most fundamental definition, and the most basic reason for the ubiquity and importance of monads. Regardless of this, the earlier more elementary definitions are both practically and pedagogically essential.
          %p
            Finally, a monad can be defined in terms of the “Kleisli operation” taking any map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \to T b
            to a map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T a \to T b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Set
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T : \mathbf{Set} \to \mathbf{Set}
            is the “free
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi TY
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to TY
            is equivalent to a linear function from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TX
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi TY
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TY
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            specifies how to map the basis vectors of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TX
            into
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi TY
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TY
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            has a unique extension to a linear function on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi TX
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TX
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TX
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TX
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TY
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TY
            specifies a unique function from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TY
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TY
            by restricting to the basis.) See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+system"} extension system
            for more details.
          %p
            We can picture a monad in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            as an image of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oriental"} third oriental
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
          %p
            The data of and axioms for a monad can be expressed graphically with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \colon C \to C, \eta, \mu
            for the monad in question (this notation being the standard one when
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo =
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K = Cat
          %p
            %img{:alt => "String diagrams of the monad data (for \"Monad\")", :src => "/nlab/files/monad-data-labeled.png"}/
          %p Thanks to the distinctive shapes, one can usually omit the labels:
          %p
            %img{:alt => "String diagrams of the monad data, unlabeled (for \"Monad\")", :src => "/nlab/files/monad-data-unlabeled.png"}/
          %p The axioms then appear as:
          %p
            %img{:alt => "String diagrams of the monad axioms, unlabeled (for \"Monad\")", :src => "/nlab/files/monad-axioms-unlabeled.png"}/
          %h3#BicategoryOfMonads The 2-category of monads
          %p
            Given the equivalence between monads in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+functors"} lax functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to K
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Bénabou67"}> Bénabou 1967, pp. 39
            \&amp;rbrack; it is straightforward to define the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
