              %p which is exactly the associativity of the multiplication of the monad.
              %p
                %strong (4)
                The naturality of the multiplication
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mrow
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(\epsilon_{F(A)}):U(F(U(F(A)))) \rightarrow U(F(A))
                is obtained by two
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/whiskerings"} whiskerings
                of the counit
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϵ
                          %mi B
                        %mo :
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_{B}:U(F(B)) \rightarrow B
            %h4#CategoryOfAdjunctionResolutionsOfAMonad Category of adjunction-resolutions of a monad
            %p
              An adjunction inducing a monad
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              (as
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#MonadInducedByAnAdjunction"} above
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  resolution of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %p
              There is in general more than one such resolution, in fact there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              of adjunctions for a given monad whose morphisms are “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comparison+functors"}> comparison functors
              ” (eg.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#MacLane71"} MacLane 1971, §VI.3
            %p In this category:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
                  is the adjunction over the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
                  of the monad;
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
                  is that over the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Moore+category"} Eilenberg-Moore category
                  of algebras, also called the
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+adjunction"} monadic adjunction
                  (recognized by
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadicity+theorems"} monadicity theorems
            %p
              (e.g.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "monad#Borceux94"} Borceux 1994, vol. 2, prop. 4.2.2
            %h4#SemanticsStructureAdjunction Semantics-structure adjunction
            %p
              The above passage from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
              to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
              and back to their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+adjunctions"} monadic adjunctions
              constitutes itself an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics-structure+adjunction"} semantics-structure adjunction
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#monads_on_
            Monads on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %p
            Many of these monads also have standard usages as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+%28computer+science%29"} monads in computer science
          #MaybeMonad.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              The free-forgetful
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+sets"} pointed sets
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              induces an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctor"} endofunctor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %mi Set
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)_* : Set \to Set
              which adds a new disjoint point. This is called the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maybe+monad"} maybe monad
              in computer science.
          #ListMonad.num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              The free-forgetful
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              induces an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctor"} endofunctor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo :
                    %mi Set
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T : Set \to Set
              defined by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi TA
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨆
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TA := \bigsqcup_{n \ge 0} A^n
            %p
              giving the
              = succeed "." do
                %strong free monoid monad
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/list+monad"} list monad
              or
              %span.newWikiWord
                Kleene-Star
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Kleene-Star"} ?
              in computer science. The components of the unit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_A : A \to T A
              give inclusions sending each element of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              to the corresponding singleton list. The components of the multiplication
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mn 2
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_A : T^2 A \to T A
              are the concatenation functions, sending a list of lists to the corresponding list (Known as flattening in computer science). This monad can be defined in any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
              that distribute over the monoidal product.
          #StateMonad.num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              For a fixed set of “states”
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              = succeed ")-adjunction" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ×
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \times - \dashv (-)^S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S \times -)^S
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              called the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state+monad"} state monad
          #ContinuationMonad.num_example
            %h6#example_4 Example
            %p
              The contravariant
              = succeed "-functor" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi P
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≅
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mi P
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Set(A,P B) \cong \Set (B, P A)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi P
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(A, P B) = \hom(A, \hom(B,\Omega)) \cong \hom( A \times B, \Omega) = P(A \times B)
              inducing a
              %strong double power set monad
              taking a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi P
                        %mn 2
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P^2 A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+a+monad"} unit
              are the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter"} principal ultrafilter
              functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn 2
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_A \colon A \to P^2 A
              which send an element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              to the set of subsets of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              that contain
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_A
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
              function for the map
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi η
                        %mrow
                          %mi P
                          %mi A
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi P
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi P
                        %mn 3
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_{P A} \colon P A \to P^3 A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              with the property that one of the sets of sets of subsets is the set of all sets of subsets of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              that include that particular subset as an element.
            %p
              Replacing the two element
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+object"} power object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              with any other set gives similar monads. In
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+in+computer+science"} computer science contexts
              these are known as
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuation+monad"} continuation monads
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} bi-closed monoidal category
              %strong double dual monad
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Vect
                          %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Vect_k
          .num_example
            %h6#example_5 Example
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+monad"} function monad
              (also “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reader+monad"}> reader monad
              ”, cf.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreader+comonad"} coreader comonad
          .num_example
            %h6#example_6 Example
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possibility"} possibility
          .num_example
            %h6#example_7 Example
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/selection+monad"} selection monad
          %h3#algebra Algebra
          #FreeRModMonad.num_example
            %h6#example_8 Example
            %p
              The free-forgetful
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              and the category of
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
              %strong
                free
                = succeed "-module" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo :
                      %mi Set
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[-] : Set \to Set
              %strong free abelian group monad
              and
              %strong free vector space monad
              are special cases.
          #FreeGroupMonad.num_example
            %h6#example_9 Example
            %p
              The free-forgetful
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              and the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
              gives the
              %strong free group monad
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi Set
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : Set \to Set
              that sends
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              to the set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(A)
              of finite words in the letters
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
              together with inverses
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a^{-1}
          %h3#topology Topology
          #UltrafilterMonad.num_example
            %h6#example_10 Example
            %p
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Top
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U : \Top \to \Set
              taking a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              to its underlying
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D: \Set \to \Top
              taking a set to its
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topology"} discrete topology
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi U
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta \dashv U'
              between the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact"} compact
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+topological+spaces"} Hausdorff topological spaces
              and the category of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
              is the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone-Cech+compactification"} Stone-Cech compactification
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta : \Set \to \Set
              sending a set to its underlying set of the Stone-Cech compactification of its discrete space. It is also known as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter"} ultrafilter
              monad as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
              can be thought of as the functor taking a set to its set of ultrafilters.
          %h3#monads_in_cat Monads in Cat
          %p
            Monads are often considered in the 2-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            where they are given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctors"} endofunctors
            with a monoid structure on them. In particular, monads
            %em in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %em on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            are equivalent to the equational theories studied in universal algebra. In this context, a monad abstracts the concept of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
            (such as “group” or “ring”), giving a general notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
            on an object of a category.
          %p
            Classically, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{T}
            is an algebraic theory (e.g. the theory of groups), a
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{T}
            %em terms
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a\cdot c)
            %em axioms
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a\cdot (b\cdot c))=((a\cdot b)\cdot c)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T X
            is the set of all terms of the theory formed from elements of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T X
            is thus the (underlying set of the)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+group"} free group
            of formal words
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi b
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \cdot b \cdot \cdots \cdot s
            from
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            gives
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+object"} free
            structures turns out to be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+adjunction"} typical
          %p
            To capture the theory fully, we need to include a little more data: a natural map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_X : X \to T X
            recording how each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in X
            gives a trivial term
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_X:T T X \to T X
            recording how further terms built from terms are already present as terms in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T X
          %p
            Given a monad in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            on a category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+resolution"} canonical resolution
            of any object of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %h3#other_examples Other examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Monads on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} posets
                are particularly simple (in particular, they are always
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} idempotent
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+closure"} Moore closure
            %li
              %p
                An
